cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this research base — its evidence graph, a study, a dataset manifest, or the
  Context Kit — please cite it. Cite individual findings by their node ID (for example F46)
  together with the study document that carries them, so the citation survives supersession.
title: "MONAD Quant: an open quantitative finance research base"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "huthargrave-lang and the MONAD Quant contributors"
repository-code: "https://github.com/huthargrave-lang/MONAD-quant"
url: "https://huthargrave-lang.github.io/MONAD-quant/"
license: MIT
version: "2026.10"
date-released: "2026-10-29"
keywords:
  - quantitative finance
  - reproducible research
  - evidence graph
  - knowledge graph
  - asset allocation
  - market microstructure
  - execution risk
  - leveraged ETFs
  - SEC filings
  - corporate actions
  - closed-end funds
  - negative results
  - pre-registration
abstract: >-
  MONAD Quant is a typed, append-only evidence graph of reproducible quantitative finance
  studies — on asset allocation, execution risk in leveraged ETFs, SEC filings and corporate
  actions, closed-end-fund discounts, commodity linkages, and the epistemics of research
  itself — together with the governance machinery that keeps the claims honest
  (pre-registration, a hash-chained trial ledger, refutation and admission gates, forward
  watches) and a stdlib-only Context Kit that agents and humans query instead of reading the
  repository. A long-only mean-reversion trading engine ships with it as the reference test
  bench the methodology was built on; the base's flagship result is the verified negative
  that this engine has no risk-adjusted edge over a static allocation.
